A||N||5||FieroSTETZ||will the v6 from a cavalier...||||1||
Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||06-11-2000||03:25 PM||||i am gonna get a fully rebuilt 2.8 from an '86 cavalier- will this engine work in my 87gt? he said &lt;&lt;The engine has the intake manifold, the heads, the<BR>pan, the starter, and a couple of sensors.  It is all<BR>rebuilt.&gt;&gt; wouldnt i need to remove things like the intake manifold and replace it with the fiero one? new sensors? would i need a new ecm? i am REALLY new to this new engine thing, any help would be great!||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroSTETZ||06-11-2000||03:25 PM||||oh, BTW, im gettin it for 250 dollars out the door, guy has to go to bootcamp||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||06-11-2000||04:08 PM||||The basic engine is the same, however accessory brackets, pulleys, and a few other odds and ends will probably be different. The heads are probably not the big valve HO heads like our Fieros. If I was doing it, I'd just use the Cav short block and have the Fiero heads rebuilt and then switch all the externally goodies off the Fiero engine onto the Cav short block. No problem with the ECM or anything else then.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||06-11-2000||05:07 PM||||Does the Cavalier have the DIS system or does it have a distributer? Some late 86's had the HO 2.8 with the DIS and aluminum heads. If this is the case, the pistons are also different.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||06-11-2000||06:24 PM||||After the carburated engines, I don't think GM made any injected engines with the smaller valves. I've seen tons of FI engines (port and TBI) and none had the smaller valves.<P>The J car's started with iron heads and a different induction to cram it under the hood. The engine was tilted too. The long block should be completely usable. Of course you could swap the whole dang thing in too.<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000005||Hokie||06-12-2000||06:09 PM||||The 86 V6 cavaliers had the HO big valve heads.  Cooter's right, if you get a V6 cavalier motor that has the splayed valve aluminum heads, the pistons will be dished.  I know that all 87 Z24's had these heads, but I'm not aware of any 86s that have them although I guess it's possible.  In this case, you have to use the aluminum heads on the cavalier motor and not the fiero heads.  This is not a bad thing, the splayed valve aluminum heads have the big valves and are better than the iron heads on our fiero's.  As far as I know, all the aluminum head motors had a DIS.  The cam still had the distributor drive gear, but I don't know how hard it would be to either attach the fiero distributor or convert the fiero to use the DIS system.  It would probably be much easier to use a cavalier motor with a distributor and the big valve iron heads.  Better make sure before you buy.||||reg||1
